      "proposition": {
        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p>Various laws and 250 years of tradition prevent the likeness of living people from appearing on currency. Donald Trump and Scott Bessent don’t want you to worry about that</p><ul><li><p>This was originally published in This Week in Trumpland. <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/apr/25/election-newsletter-sign-up-stakes\">Sign up to receive it in your inbox every Wednesday</a></p></li></ul><p>Two hallmarks of <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump\">Donald Trump</a>’s second-term: the president’s enthusiasm for putting his name and face on things, and his cavalier approach to established law.</p><p>Today the president is once again combining both those interests, as the government releases a <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jul/15/trump-coin-us-mint-production\">$1 coin bearing his image</a>, an item of currency that scholars say should be illegal, but which is happening anyway. How many times have you heard that in the last 18 months?</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/02/trump-one-dollar-coin\">Continue reading...</a>"
